App 28: The Instrument Provenance Registry
Completeness status: COMPLETE. All sections delivered in one pass. No truncation. Formatted for copy-paste. Long lists are broken into short lines.

1. What It Is
A provenance-aware, versioned database of scientific instruments. A scientific instrument is any device or procedure used to make a measurement: a telescope, a spectrometer, a particle detector, a survey questionnaire, a sequencing machine, a thermometer, an economic index. The Instrument Provenance Registry records each instrument with its measurement kernel, its selection function, its calibration history, its known biases, and its provenance.

It reuses:

the Effective-Null Builder (App 11)

the Provenance-Aware Annotation Database (App 20)

the Numerical Verification Tool (App 22)

the PROV-O Exporter (App 3)

the Revision History Viewer (App 7)

the Physics Knowledge Library (App 23)

the Autofill Library Service (App 49)

It is for experimentalists, metrologists, survey designers, astronomers, and anyone who has to combine measurements from different instruments.

2. Core Requirements
Functional requirements:

Accept deposits of instruments from users.

Store each instrument with its name, its type, its field, its measurement kernel, its selection function, and its provenance.

Record the calibration history: each calibration with its date, its source, and its correction.

Record the known biases: each bias with its description, its magnitude, and its source.

Record the measurement kernel: the probability density of observing a value given the true value.

Record the selection function: the probability that an observation enters the sample.

Record the instrument's operational range: the values over which the instrument is valid.

Record the instrument's resolution: the smallest difference the instrument can distinguish.

Record the instrument's precision: the repeatability of a measurement.

Record the instrument's accuracy: the closeness to the true value.

Record the instrument's uncertainties: the sources and magnitudes of the uncertainties.

Record the provenance: the manufacturer, the model, the serial number, the date of manufacture, and the date of first use.

Enforce provenance completeness.

Enforce versioning.

Enforce supersession acyclicity.

Support querying by instrument ID, name, type, field, subfield, manufacturer, model, depositor, deposit date, and keywords.

Support full-text search of the instrument description, the calibration notes, and the bias notes.

Support aggregation queries: count by type, by field, by manufacturer, by year, by depositor.

Support exporting an instrument as JSON, PROV-O, and Turtle.

Support importing an instrument from JSON.

Support bulk import and bulk export.

Support a REST API for CRUD operations.

Support a GraphQL API for flexible queries.

Support a command-line interface for administration.

Support authentication and authorization.

Support audit logging.

Support backup and restore.

Support a web interface for depositing, browsing, and querying.

Support moderation.

Support linking an instrument to a STAG annotation if the instrument is part of a scale-transition claim.

Support linking an instrument to a numerical verification result if the instrument's values have been verified.

Support linking an instrument to a Physics Knowledge Library work if the instrument is described in a deposited work.

Support linking an instrument to a dataset if the instrument produced the dataset.

Support the instrument comparison view: a visualisation that compares two or more instruments by their measurement kernels and selection functions.

Support the calibration timeline: a visualisation of the calibration history of an instrument.

Support the bias map: a visualisation of the known biases of an instrument across its operational range.

Non-functional requirements:

Deterministic.

Fast: query a typical instrument in under 100 milliseconds; aggregate over 10,000 instruments in under one second.

Scalable: support up to 10^6 instruments.

Versioned: the registry records the STAG schema version, the instrument schema version, and the registry version.

Reproducible: the registry records the provenance for every deposit.

Portable.

Well-documented.

